  "summary_md": "Communication equipment and industrial machinery names are pivoting aggressively toward defense, AI infrastructure, and higher-value solutions while shedding commoditized or legacy lines. The batch’s boldest strategic resets come from BWEN, exiting wind-tower manufacturing in a market-rewarded move, and INSG, whose pending Nokia acquisition aims to create a global wireless edge player. Defense demand is a dominant thread: LASE secured a U.S. Department of War anti-drone system selection and a J&J medical order, while FEIM’s record space-defense backlog underpins a goal to double revenue—TWIN and TAYD also cite defense tailwinds. AI fiber and data-center connectivity are lifting CLFD and OCC, though OCC draws heavy short-seller skepticism.\n\nA margin-over-volume theme runs through LXFR and NTGR, where cost discipline and mix shifts boost profitability even as sales lag. The batch also highlights an active M&A and divestiture cycle—CMTL sold its satellite segment, BURU acquired Tekne to enter defense, and MITQ integrated a loudspeaker acquisition—as management teams refocus portfolios amid uneven end-market recoveries and persistent skepticism toward unproven transformations.",
  "summary_date": "2026-06-28"
